 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A business exchanges with partners (suppliers, customers, employees, the bank, the State). Each exchange is an economic flow: something moving from one partner to another.\n• Real (physical) flows: goods or services (goods delivered, work done, a repair).\n• Financial (monetary) flows: money (cash, cheques, transfers, loans).\nMost transactions have two flows in opposite directions: when a shop buys goods for cash, goods flow in (real) and money flows out (financial).\nSome flows have no counterpart at once (a credit purchase creates a debt; the payment comes later).\nAccounting analysis means studying each transaction to find its flows, the partners involved, and the accounts affected.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "t": "a shop buys goods worth 200 000 FCFA from a supplier and pays cash"
  },
  "problem": "Analyse the flows when {t}.",
  "steps": [
   "Real flow: goods move from the supplier into the shop.",
   "Financial flow: cash moves from the shop to the supplier.",
   "Two flows in opposite directions, of equal value."
  ],
  "answer": "Goods in (real), cash out (financial).",
  "checks": []
 },
